    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gateway-of-the-sun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gateway-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="Gateway of the Sun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gateway-of-the-sun">Gateway of the Sun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zonnepoort_tiwanaku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mhwater, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wari took shape in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> basin of the central Peruvian highlands, where farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huarpa-culture">Huarpa culture</a> had terraced and irrigated the valley slopes during the first half of the first millennium CE. Around 600 a new polity emerged there, building on Huarpa traditions, on contacts with the Nasca peoples of the south coast, whose polychrome pottery influenced early Wari styles, and on religious imagery shared with Tiwanaku near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>. At Conchopata, now within the city of Ayacucho, people deliberately smashed giant urns painted with a front-facing Staff God flanked by winged attendants, figures very close to those on Tiwanaku’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gateway-of-the-sun">Gateway of the Sun</a>, although how the two traditions were connected is still debated. The capital, Wari or Huari, grew on a plateau north-east of modern Ayacucho, near the town of Quinua. Its core of walled compounds, temples and underground galleries covered several square kilometres, and estimates of its population at its height range from about 30,000 to 70,000. Radiocarbon studies place the consolidation of state authority in the Ayacucho region in the seventh century, followed soon afterwards by expansion, during the period archaeologists call the Middle Horizon. Because Wari left no writing, its origins are known almost entirely from archaeology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cerro-baul.jpg" alt="Cerro Baúl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerro_Baul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">simon_chara, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wari’s economy rested on highland farming. Its settlers spread terraced and irrigated fields through the valleys they occupied: in the Carhuarazo valley, Katharina Schreiber found that Wari rule brought new terraces and a shift from tubers alone to tubers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. Llamas and alpacas supplied wool, meat and transport, and feasting deposits suggest that camelids were also prized as a form of wealth. Maize beer, or chicha, and a spicy beer brewed from the berries of the molle tree were central to political life. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> valley, archaeologists led by Patrick Ryan Williams found a brewery with more than twenty vats, where Wari lords probably hosted feasts that bound local leaders to the state. Craft production reached a high standard. Workshops at Conchopata fired large polychrome vessels in kilns, and weavers made tapestry tunics with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> warps and dyed camelid-fibre wefts, some woven at more than 100 threads to the inch, whose standardised, deliberately compressed designs probably came from state-sponsored workshops. Metalworkers used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, gold and arsenical bronze. Society was strongly ranked: large elite compounds and richly furnished tombs contrast with simple burials, and the mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-de-huarmey">El Castillo de Huarmey</a> shows that noblewomen could command great wealth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pikillacta.jpg" alt="Pikillacta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piquillacta_Archaeological_site_-_street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AgainErick, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No written record describes Wari’s wars, so its military history must be read from sites, art and human remains, and scholars still debate how far its expansion relied on force rather than colonisation, alliance and religion. Some provincial centres were clearly built with security in mind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> stands on a flat-topped mountain with steep sides above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> valley, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, enclosed by high walls, lay in a basin whose entrances appear to have been fortified. Human remains give the clearest evidence of violence. Tiffiny Tung found head wounds on 26 per cent of adults at Conchopata and on about a third of those at Beringa in the Majes valley and at La Real, rates higher than in several other Andean populations, though she cautions that not all of this violence was military. In ritual buildings at Conchopata she identified trophy heads, perforated for a carrying cord; chemical analysis showed that most of those tested were not local, and several were the heads of children, which suggests raids on distant communities. Yet Wari did not always meet rivals with force. In the Moquegua valley, Wari colonists on Cerro Baúl and Tiwanaku settlers on the valley floor lived within sight of each other for centuries without clear signs of war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/viracochapampa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viracochapampa.jpg" alt="Viracochapampa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/viracochapampa">Viracochapampa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiracochapampa1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trabajo propio, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wari’s rulers are unknown by name, and its government must be inferred from its buildings. Its hallmark was a standardised architecture of large rectangular enclosures divided into patio groups, rooms arranged around square courts, laid out on rigid grids. The same plan appears at the capital and at provincial centres hundreds of kilometres apart: Jincamocco in the Carhuarazo valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a> in the Lucre basin south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracochapampa">Viracochapampa</a> near Huamachuco in the north, which was never finished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a>. Such uniformity suggests central planning and officials sent out from the core, but the density of Wari presence varied greatly, and in many regions local lords probably kept their power under Wari oversight. D-shaped temples, found at the capital, at Conchopata and as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a>, linked these centres to a state religion. Wari had no writing, but cords with knots and coloured wrappings, early forms of the khipus later used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, have been radiocarbon-dated to between the late eighth and early eleventh centuries, and a 2024 study of some 370 radiocarbon dates suggests that such record-keeping and roads for runners developed relatively late in Wari’s history. Underground stone galleries at the capital, looted before archaeologists reached them, probably held royal burials.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacamac.jpg" alt="Pachacamac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pachacamac_-_51163366566.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wari is often described as the first empire of the Andes, and many techniques of rule that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> used five centuries later had Wari precedents. Administrative centres on standard plans, state-sponsored feasting with chicha, terracing spread into new valleys, roads linking provinces and records kept on knotted cords all appear in Wari contexts, and several Wari provincial sites lie along routes later followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan">Inca road system</a>. The Inca heartland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> had itself held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a> and other Wari settlements, though how much the Incas inherited directly remains debated. Wari styles spread across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>: its tunics, ceramics and Staff God imagery influenced coastal societies, and the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> on the central coast flourished under Wari influence while keeping much of its own identity. Some linguists have argued that Wari expansion helped spread early forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a>, a hypothesis that remains unproven. Excavations have transformed knowledge of Wari in recent decades. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a> in the Vilcabamba forests, later the last refuge of the Incas, Peruvian archaeologists working from 2010 found the tomb of a Wari lord with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mask and breastplate, and the unlooted mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-de-huarmey">El Castillo de Huarmey</a>, announced in 2013, revealed the wealth of Wari noblewomen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wari’s power faded during the tenth and eleventh centuries, and by about 1000 its network of provinces had broken apart, although the process was gradual and is still imprecisely dated. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> the colonists held a last feast, then burned the brewery and palaces and threw their drinking cups into the flames before leaving, around 1000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a> was abandoned and later swept by fire, and at the capital the doorways of some compounds were deliberately blocked, as if their users intended to return. Radiocarbon dates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> suggest that some authority persisted at the capital until about the mid-eleventh century, while rural villages carried on with their own pottery traditions. The causes of the collapse are debated. Some palaeoclimate records point to drier conditions in parts of the Andes around the end of the first millennium, which would have strained the farming and feasting on which Wari politics depended, while other scholars stress political strife and the cost of holding distant provinces. Skeletons from the Ayacucho region show more violent injuries after the collapse, as the Andes entered the Late Intermediate Period of small rival polities. Groups later known as the Chanka occupied the Ayacucho region, while the Kingdom of Chimor rose on the north coast.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Often regarded as the first Andean empire, Wari ruled highland and coastal provinces between about 600 and 1000 through planned administrative centres, feasting, colonies and knotted-cord records, and its methods of rule foreshadowed those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> five centuries later.</em></p>

